WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.439
MCP audit trails should make tool-mediated activity understandable.

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:08.030
Useful logs include tool calls, selected server, calling user, application identity,

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:11.786
authorization decision, approval record, resource access event, policy denial,

00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:14.708
server connection event, error, timing, and outcome.

00:00:14.708 --> 00:00:15.960
For high-impact actions,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.803
logs should preserve enough context to reconstruct who requested the action, what was approved,

00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:25.559
what arguments were used, and what downstream system responded.

00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:29.733
Monitoring should look for unusual tool usage, suspicious server behavior,

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:34.741
data access anomalies, repeated denials, unexpected local file access, unusual API calls,

00:00:34.741 --> 00:00:39.332
elevated error rates, approval bypass attempts, or activity outside normal patterns.

00:00:39.332 --> 00:00:43.506
The goal is not to drown teams in model transcripts.

00:00:43.506 --> 00:00:48.514
The goal is to expose the security-relevant events that show whether tools

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:51.853
and resources are being used according to policy.

00:00:51.853 --> 00:00:56.862
Logs also create privacy and security risk if they store too much.

00:00:56.862 --> 00:01:00.618
They should avoid unnecessary secrets, tokens, credentials, regulated data,

00:01:00.618 --> 00:01:03.122
personal information, and sensitive user content.

00:01:03.122 --> 00:01:06.461
Correlation IDs, resource identifiers, tool names, policy outcomes,

00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:13.139
and carefully scoped summaries can often support investigation without storing full prompts or full resource contents.

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.226
Logging design should be intentional,

00:01:15.226 --> 00:01:20.651
because incident response is harder when logs are either empty or dangerously over-collected.

00:01:20.651 --> 00:01:25.242
Incident response plans should cover unauthorized actions, data leakage, tool poisoning,

00:01:25.242 --> 00:01:29.833
malicious server activity, policy bypass, approval confusion, and suspicious resource access.

00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:34.842
Responders need to know how to disable a tool, revoke a token,

00:01:34.842 --> 00:01:39.015
block a server, preserve evidence, identify affected users or tenants,

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:41.520
review downstream actions, and communicate impact.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.780
MCP security is strongest when monitoring and response are designed before the first serious incident.
